import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Callout
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero

meta = {
	title: "Actors",
	description: "Actors on the Roc Solid server platform: state that handles one message at a time, in the order they came, with typed methods, asks, tells and timers. Its state and timers are kept in the database, so they outlive a restart.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "kinds", label: "Kinds and methods" },
		{ id: "asking", label: "Asking and telling" },
		{ id: "timers", label: "Timers" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not yet" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The platform · actors"
				title="State, one message at a time"
			>
				A shopping cart, a counter, a game room: state that many requests change, and that must stay
				right when they come at once. An <strong>actor</strong> holds such state and handles one message
				at a time, in the order they came, so a hundred requests can change it together and none loses
				another's update. Its state is kept in the database, so it is still there after a restart. For
				what only matters right now (who is online, what was just said), use
				<a href={Paths.platform_live}>live state</a> in memory instead.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="kinds">Kinds and methods</Heading>
			<p>
				An actor is a <strong>kind</strong> and a <strong>key</strong>: the cart of member 7, the counter
				called <code>visits</code>. A kind gives its name and its starting state (<code>initial</code>,
				computed from the key). What its actors can do are its <strong>methods</strong>, each with its own
				input and reply types. A method's turn is given the actor (<code>me</code>), the app's context
				(what <code>init!</code> made), the actor's current state and the input, and returns the next
				state and the reply.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_counter.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_counter.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Nobody creates or ends an actor.</strong> A call to any key finds that actor as its last
					turn left it, or new: <code>initial(key)</code> is where its state starts.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>One turn at a time, in order.</strong> Each actor has a mailbox in the server and takes
					what is in it one turn after another, whichever method each message is for. A hundred asks at
					once each see the state the one before left: no lost updates, and no locks for you to write.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A bad call is an error, not a crash.</strong> Methods are typed, so this is rare: a caller
					built against an older or newer kind may send an input a method doesn't take, or name a method
					it doesn't have. That, a crash inside a method, and a kind the app doesn't have are each logged
					as an error and reported to whoever asked. The actor's state stays as it was, and it goes on
					with its next turn.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Kept in the database</strong> (<code>rocx_actors</code>): read by an actor's first turn,
					and written after each turn that changes it. An actor idle for 15 minutes
					(<code>--actor-idle-ms</code>) is let go from memory, and the next call wakes it as the database
					has it.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				<strong>How the types fit together.</strong> A kind is a type of your app's that has
				<code>name</code> and <code>initial</code> (Roc checks this through <code>Actor.Kind</code>). It
				names the type of its methods once,
				<code>{"Method(input, reply) : Actor.Method(Counter, Str, Str, { count : I64 }, input, reply)"}</code>:
				the kind, the app's context, the key and the state. Each method is then a value,
				<code>{"Actor.method(name, run!)"}</code>, annotated with that type, such as
				<code>{"Counter.Method(I64, I64)"}</code>, which is what makes it that kind's.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Keys.</strong> A key is any type with <code>to_str</code>, which names its actor (in the
				database too), and <code>from_quote</code>, which reads that name back and lets a string literal be
				a key (<code>Actor.Keyed</code>). <code>Str</code> has both, and so can a type of your own, such as
				<code>{"[YouTube, Instagram(Str)]"}</code>. A method is given the key as its own type
				(<code>me.key()</code>), so it can match on it rather than parse it, and a key of another kind's
				type doesn't compile.
			</p>
			<p>
				<code>rocx build</code> finds the methods your modules define (<code>Actor.method</code>, at the top
				of a module's block) and lists them in the program, so the server can run an actor when nobody is
				waiting on it: for a tell, or a timer. It also checks that each kind's name
				(<code>{"name = |{}| \"…\""}</code>) is unique. Two methods of one kind with the same name stop the
				server as it starts.
			</p>

			<Heading id="asking">Asking and telling</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_ask.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_ask.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>{"Counter.add.ask!(key, input)"}</code> waits for the reply, typed as the method says, or
				<code>ActorErr(why)</code> when there is none. <code>{"Counter.add.tell!(key, input)"}</code> doesn't
				wait: its turn comes after the ones before it, and its reply goes nowhere (a problem is logged).
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_tell.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_tell.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				An actor may ask another actor. An ask that would wait forever is refused at once: an actor asking
				itself, or asking any actor whose turn is waiting on its answer. An ask not answered within 30
				seconds is given up on, and its message is dropped if its turn hasn't come yet.
			</p>

			<Heading id="timers">Timers</Heading>
			<p>
				A method is given the actor itself, <code>me</code>: its key (<code>me.key()</code>) and its own
				timers. <code>{"me.after!(ms, method, input)"}</code> calls one of its kind's methods later (one of
				another kind's doesn't compile) and returns the timer, which <code>Actor.cancel!</code> stops. For
				something every so often, let each call set the next. A method that names itself is written as a
				function of <code>{"{}"}</code>, because a value can't be defined in terms of itself:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_ticker.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_ticker.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>{"Counter.add.tell_after!(key, ms, input)"}</code> does the same from anywhere: a request, a
				job, another actor.
			</p>
			<p>
				With a database, a timer outlives a restart. It is a row in <code>rocx_actor_timers</code>, written
				at once (outside any transaction the caller has open, just as a tell is sent at once) and deleted
				once the turn that handled it has ended; the server sets every row again as it starts. So a timer's
				call is made at least once: a server killed in the middle of that turn makes it again after the
				restart. <code>Actor.cancel!</code> stops a timer that is waiting, or one that has fired but whose
				turn hasn't come. Without a database, timers are in memory, and a restart loses them.
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits">Not yet</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>An actor's state is JSON in one row</strong>, written after each message that changes it:
					fine for a cart, not for a ledger.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Tells are in memory.</strong> A restart loses those not yet handled; use a
					<a href={Paths.ecosystem_jobs}>job</a> for work that must survive one. Timers do survive it, with
					a database.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Rooms, topics, channels, values in memory, actors, tables and jobs, side by side:
				<a href={"${Paths.platform_live}#which"}>which to use</a>.
			</p>
			<Callout kind="tip" title="Tested">
				<p>
					The platform's tests ask an actor a hundred times at once, crash one, have one ask another,
					itself and one waiting on it, tell one in order, set and cancel timers, send one a message it
					doesn't take, let one go when idle and wake it, and restart the server to find an actor's state
					where it was and its timer fired once after the start.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
